    <article class="doc">
        <h2 id="file">Configuration file</h2>

        <p>
            The default file is <code>/etc/mkinitramfs.conf</code>. It is
            sourced as POSIX shell at the beginning of image generation. Use
            <code>-c &lt;file&gt;</code> to load another file. The file should
            contain assignments only; do not put destructive commands in it.
        </p>

        <h2 id="variables">Variables</h2>

        <table>
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th>Variable</th>
                    <th>Value format</th>
                    <th>Meaning</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>

            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <td><code>INITFSCOMP</code></td>

                    <td>
                        <code>gzip</code>, <code>xz</code>, <code>zstd</code>,
                        <code>lz4</code>, <code>none</code>
                    </td>

                    <td>
                        Compression used for the cpio stream. Default is
                        <code>gzip</code>.
                    </td>
                </tr>

                <tr>
                    <td><code>HOOKS</code></td>
                    <td>space-separated names</td>

                    <td>
                        Default hooks, in build and boot order. Each name
                        resolves to <code>name.hook</code>.
                    </td>
                </tr>

                <tr>
                    <td><code>BINARIES</code></td>
                    <td>space-separated commands or paths</td>
                    <td>Extra binaries; shared-library dependencies are added automatically.</td>
                </tr>

                <tr>
                    <td><code>MODULES</code></td>
                    <td>space-separated module names</td>

                    <td>
                        Extra kernel modules; dependencies and firmware are
                        resolved through <code>modinfo</code>.
                    </td>
                </tr>

                <tr>
                    <td><code>FILES</code></td>
                    <td>space-separated absolute paths</td>
                    <td>Extra files copied into the image.</td>
                </tr>

                <tr>
                    <td><code>INITRAMFS</code></td>
                    <td>output path</td>

                    <td>
                        Default output image path. The command-line
                        <code>-o</code> option takes precedence.
                    </td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <h2 id="hooks">Hook discovery</h2>

        <p>
            Hook names are searched through <code>HOOKDIRS</code>, a
            colon-separated search path. The installed default directory is
            <code>/usr/share/mkinitramfs/hooks</code>. An entry
            <code>encrypt</code> resolves to
            <code>/usr/share/mkinitramfs/hooks/encrypt.hook</code>. An absolute
            path may be passed through <code>-A</code> or <code>-a</code>.
        </p>

        <pre><code>HOOKS="base modules udev"
# Example custom search path for one invocation:
HOOKDIRS="/etc/mkinitramfs/hooks:/usr/share/mkinitramfs/hooks"</code></pre>

        <h2 id="overrides">Command-line precedence</h2>

        <table>
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th>Option</th>
                    <th>Effect</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>

            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <td><code>-C &lt;compression&gt;</code></td>
                    <td>Replace <code>INITFSCOMP</code> for this build.</td>
                </tr>

                <tr>
                    <td><code>-A &lt;hooks&gt;</code></td>
                    <td>Prepend hooks before configured hooks; highest precedence.</td>
                </tr>

                <tr>
                    <td>
                        <code>-s &lt;hooks&gt;</code>
                    </td>

                    <td>Remove named hooks from <code>HOOKS</code>.</td>
                </tr>

                <tr>
                    <td>
                        <code>-a &lt;hooks&gt;</code>
                    </td>

                    <td>Append hooks after the configured list and after skipped hooks are removed.</td>
                </tr>

                <tr>
                    <td>
                        <code>-m &lt;modules&gt;</code>
                    </td>

                    <td>Add comma-separated modules.</td>
                </tr>

                <tr>
                    <td>
                        <code>-b &lt;binaries&gt;</code>
                    </td>

                    <td>Add comma-separated binaries.</td>
                </tr>

                <tr>
                    <td>
                        <code>-f &lt;files&gt;</code>
                    </td>

                    <td>Add comma-separated files.</td>
                </tr>

                <tr>
                    <td>
                        <code>-o &lt;output&gt;</code>
                    </td>

                    <td>Replace the configured output path.</td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>
            The effective sequence is: load configuration, remove hooks selected
            by <code>-s</code>, place <code>-A</code> hooks before the remaining
            list, then append <code>-a</code> hooks. Hook order controls both
            build-time and boot-time execution.
        </p>

        <h2 id="example">Complete example</h2>

        <pre><code>INITFSCOMP="zstd"
HOOKS="base modules udev encrypt"
BINARIES="bash"
MODULES="nvme ext4 dm_mod"
FILES="/etc/crypttab /etc/modprobe.d/storage.conf"
INITRAMFS="/boot/initrd-linux.img"

# Build using the configured defaults:
mkinitramfs

# Or override only selected values:
mkinitramfs -C xz -s encrypt -a automod -o /boot/initrd-safe.img</code></pre>

        <p>
            Keep the system configuration conservative. Add a hook only when the
            initramfs genuinely needs the payload or behavior; unnecessary
            binaries and rules increase image size and early-boot complexity.
        </p>
    </article>
</section>
